module

public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.SymmetricAlgebra.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Quotient.Operations

/-!
# Symmetric algebra of a quotient module

Source: literature/stacks-project/algebra.tex, Algebra, Lemma 00DO
(lemma-presentation-sym-exterior, line 2241)
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-symmetric-algebra-quotient-module).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

/-- (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-symmetric-algebra-quotient-module) Let `R` be a
commutative ring and `p : N →ₗ[R] M` a surjective map of `R`-modules. The
symmetric algebra map induced by `p` is surjective, its kernel is the ideal
generated by the canonical images of `ker p`, and the resulting quotient is
isomorphic to the symmetric algebra of `M`.

Source: literature/stacks-project/algebra.tex, Algebra, Lemma 00DO
(lemma-presentation-sym-exterior), summed over all degrees.

Proof:
1. Apply the universal property
   (`lem-weil-pbc-symmetric-algebra-universal-property`) to `ι R M` composed with
   `p`; this constructs the unique induced algebra map and its equation on generators.
2. Apply Stacks Lemma 00DO (`lem-weil-pbc-symmetric-degree-exact`) to the exact sequence
   `ker p → N → M → 0`. In every positive degree the induced map is surjective,
   and its kernel is the image of `ker p` tensored with the preceding homogeneous piece,
   under multiplication by the canonical image of `ker p`.
3. Sum over the homogeneous pieces. Degree zero is the identity on `R`, hence has
   zero kernel. This gives surjectivity of the whole map. Its kernel is exactly the
   generated ideal: products of a kernel generator and a homogeneous element give the
   positive-degree images, and elements of the ideal are finite sums of these products.
4. The first isomorphism theorem for commutative `R`-algebras gives the quotient
   equivalence, whose composite with the quotient map is the induced algebra map.
-/
theorem symmetricAlgebra_quotient_module
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R]
    (N : Type v) (M : Type w) [AddCommGroup N] [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module R N] [Module R M]
    (p : N →ₗ[R] M) (hp : Function.Surjective p) :
    Function.Surjective
        (SymmetricAlgebra.lift ((SymmetricAlgebra.ι R M).comp p)) ∧
      RingHom.ker (SymmetricAlgebra.lift
        ((SymmetricAlgebra.ι R M).comp p)).toRingHom =
        Ideal.span (SymmetricAlgebra.ι R N '' (LinearMap.ker p : Set N)) ∧
      ∃ e :
          (SymmetricAlgebra R N ⧸
            Ideal.span (SymmetricAlgebra.ι R N '' (LinearMap.ker p : Set N)))
            ≃ₐ[R] SymmetricAlgebra R M,
        e.toAlgHom.comp (Ideal.Quotient.mkₐ R
          (Ideal.span (SymmetricAlgebra.ι R N '' (LinearMap.ker p : Set N)))) =
          SymmetricAlgebra.lift ((SymmetricAlgebra.ι R M).comp p) := by
  sorry
